  "nl_text": "This database schema, named rschema_0194, is used to store information about a construction project management system. It consists of five tables: Supplier, Project, Material, Supplier_Credit_Assessment, and Procurement_Record.\nThe Supplier table contains information about the suppliers involved in the project, including their address, email, name, and telephone number. The Supplier_ID is the primary key for this table, and it is used to link to the Supplier_Credit_Assessment table.\nThe Project table contains information about the project itself, including its name, location, start and expected completion dates, project budget, and project ID. The Project_ID is the primary key for this table, and it is used to link to the Procurement_Record table.\nThe Material table contains information about the materials used in the project, including their name, description, and material ID. The Material_ID is the primary key for this table, and it is used to link to the Procurement_Record table.\nThe Supplier_Credit_Assessment table contains information about the credit assessment of each supplier, including the assessment content, date, score, and supplier ID. The Assessment_ID is the primary key for this table, and it is used to link to the Supplier table.\nThe Procurement_Record table contains information about the procurement of materials, including the material ID, quantity, unit price, purchase date, supplier ID, and total price. The Procurement_Record_ID is the primary key for this table, and it is used to link to the Material table and Supplier table.\nThe schema also includes foreign key constraints to ensure referential integrity, such as the foreign key from Supplier_Credit_Assessment to Supplier and Procurement_Record to Project and Supplier. The update and delete rules for these foreign keys are set to CASCADE, meaning that if a supplier or project is deleted, any related records in the Supplier_Credit_Assessment and Procurement_Record tables will also be deleted.\nOverall, this schema provides a comprehensive and well-structured way to store and manage information about a construction project management system, with clear relationships between the different tables and a clear primary key for each table.",
  "gold_question": "A construction installation company has multiple suppliers, each of which has a Supplier ID, name, telephone, email, and address. The company undertakes multiple installation projects, each requiring the procurement of materials from suppliers. The procurement records must include Project ID, Supplier ID, purchase date, material name, material quantity, unit price of the material, and total price. The Supplier Management Information System shall be capable of managing procurement records and assessing supplier credibility.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0194.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0194\nCREATE TABLE `supplier` (\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `telephone` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`supplier_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `project` (\n  `project_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expected_completion_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `budget` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`project_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `material` (\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`material_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `supplier_credit_assessment` (\n  `assessment_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `assessment_content` text NOT NULL,\n  `date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `score` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`assessment_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `supplier_credit_assessment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier` (`supplier_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `procurement_record` (\n  `procurement_record_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `material_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `unit_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `purchase_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `supplier_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `total_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `project_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`procurement_record_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `procurement_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`material_id`) REFERENCES `material` (`material_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `procurement_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`supplier_id`) REFERENCES `supplier` (`supplier_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `procurement_record_ibfk_3` FOREIGN KEY (`project_id`) REFERENCES `project` (`project_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
